---
import ProjectLayout from '../../layouts/ProjectLayout.astro';
import SectionHead from '../../components/SectionHead.astro';
import Panel from '../../components/Panel.astro';
import '../../styles/cloud.css';
// Day-job facts come from the résumé (web PDF, 2026-09-08), sanitized: no
// employer name, no environment details beyond tool names. The three demos are
// illustrative of the mechanics; each one says so.
---

<ProjectLayout
  title="Cloud security"
  path="/projects/cloud-security/"
  description="Cloud security case studies from a Fortune 500 insurer: 1,100+ GCP projects mapped to owners, a 46-control container security RACI, Wiz findings routed into ServiceNow, a SOAR proof of concept, and prevention through Organization Policy."
  lede="Cloud Security Analyst at a Fortune 500 insurer since April 2026, after its IT security rotational program (June 2024 to April 2026). Sanitized case studies: no employer name, and no environment details beyond the tool names."
  tags={['GCP', 'Wiz (CSPM)', 'ServiceNow', 'Torq / Tines', 'Org Policy', 'GRC']}
  kicker="Day job · sanitized"
  headline="Cloud security, at scale"
  accent="at scale"
  slug="cloud-security"
  stats={[
    { n: '1,100+', l: 'GCP projects mapped to their owning application' },
    { n: '46', l: 'container controls in a RACI agreed with 3 teams' },
    { n: '8', l: 'missing service entries created so every project had an owner' },
    { n: 'hours → minutes', l: 'weekly security metrics reporting' },
  ]}
  demonstrates="GCP governance at scale (CMDB ownership for 1,100+ projects), CSPM operations (Wiz findings routed into ServiceNow with owner and context), SOAR evaluation (Torq, Tines), preventive controls (GCP Organization Policy), container security operating models (RACI), GRC and audit readiness, and reporting automation."
>
  <SectionHead label="Case study 01" title="Container security" />
  <p>
    <strong>In one line:</strong> I defined who owns what for container
    security: a RACI across 46 controls, agreed with Cloud Engineering,
    Networking and AppSec.
  </p>
  <ul class="cs-facts">
    <li>
      <strong>The operating model:</strong> every container control has a team
      that is responsible, one that is accountable, and the teams to consult or
      inform, so a finding never stalls on "whose is this?".
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Remediation follow-through:</strong> when unapproved pre-hardened
      images were found heading toward production, I assisted with remediation
      management: I handled the ticket and tracked it until the fix was done.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <SectionHead label="Case study 02" title="Cloud asset governance" />
  <p>
    <strong>In one line:</strong> a finding is only actionable if it can reach
    an owner, so I validated 1,100+ GCP projects and linked each one to its
    business application in the ServiceNow CMDB.
  </p>
  <p>
    Every cell below is one GCP project that existed with no reliable link to
    the business application it belonged to. Not a rounding error, not a
    backlog: this is the estate. A finding on any one of them had nowhere to
    route, because nothing recorded who owned it.
  </p>

  <Panel label="estate · 1,100 unmapped projects" status="one cell = one project">
    <div class="field-wrap">
      <canvas
        class="field"
        data-field
        data-total="1100"
        data-cols="50"
        data-special="8"
        data-mode="fill"
        role="img"
        aria-label="A field of 1,100 cells, one per unmapped GCP project, filling in as each is validated and mapped to its owning application"
      ></canvas>

      <div class="field-readout">
        <span class="field-count" data-field-count>0 / 1,100</span>
        <span data-field-label="validated and mapped to an owning application">
          validated and mapped to an owning application
        </span>
        <span class="sep"></span>
        <button class="cs-run" type="button" data-field-run>▶ map the estate</button>
      </div>

      <p class="field-note" data-field-note hidden>
        The eight lighter cells stand for the 8 Technical Service entries that
        did not exist and had to be created before their projects had an owner
        to point at. Unglamorous work, and everything below depends on it: a
        finding cannot route to an owner that was never written down.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Panel>

  <SectionHead label="Case study 03" title="Findings that arrive routed" />
  <p>
    <strong>In one line:</strong> Wiz CSPM findings now open ServiceNow tickets
    that already name the asset's owner and carry its context.
  </p>
  <ul class="cs-facts">
    <li>
      <strong>Integration:</strong> Wiz findings flow into ServiceNow
      vulnerability ticketing, pre-filled with asset ownership and context, so
      remediation is tracked from the moment a finding exists.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Intake:</strong> I co-built the ServiceNow intake process for
      cloud security requests, so asks reach the team in one tracked queue.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <SectionHead label="Case study 04" title="SOAR proof of concept" />
  <p>
    <strong>In one line:</strong> I evaluated two no-code SOAR platforms (Torq,
    Tines), built workflows that enrich a cloud vulnerability alert the moment it
    arrives, and presented them to leadership with an adoption roadmap.
  </p>
  <p>
    An alert on its own says something is wrong in a project named after a
    number. Who owns it, how bad it is and whether it is already open is
    research someone does before the real work starts. Fire one and watch the
    payload on the right grow at every step.
  </p>

  <Panel label="torq · automation run" status="step through it">
    <div class="flow-demo" data-flow>
      <div class="map-controls">
        <span class="ctl-label">run it</span>
        <div class="cs-seg" role="group" aria-label="Who handles the alert">
          <button class="cs-opt" type="button" data-flow-mode="auto" aria-pressed="true">automated · Torq</button>
          <button class="cs-opt" type="button" data-flow-mode="manual" aria-pressed="false">by hand · an analyst</button>
        </div>
        <span class="sep"></span>
        <button class="cs-run" type="button" data-flow-run>▶ raise a finding</button>
      </div>

      <svg viewBox="0 0 900 120" data-flow-svg role="img" aria-label="A five step enrichment workflow: Wiz finding, CMDB lookup, context enrichment, deduplication, case creation">
        <path class="flow-edge" d="M 180 62 H 195"></path>
        <path class="flow-edge" d="M 355 62 H 370"></path>
        <path class="flow-edge" d="M 530 62 H 545"></path>
        <path class="flow-edge" d="M 705 62 H 720"></path>

        <g class="flow-node">
          <rect x="20" y="34" width="160" height="56" rx="7"></rect>
          <text x="100" y="58" text-anchor="middle">Wiz finding</text>
          <text class="fn-sub" x="100" y="74" text-anchor="middle">trigger</text>
        </g>
        <g class="flow-node">
          <rect x="195" y="34" width="160" height="56" rx="7"></rect>
          <text x="275" y="58" text-anchor="middle">CMDB lookup</text>
          <text class="fn-sub" x="275" y="74" text-anchor="middle">who owns this?</text>
        </g>
        <g class="flow-node">
          <rect x="370" y="34" width="160" height="56" rx="7"></rect>
          <text x="450" y="58" text-anchor="middle">Enrich</text>
          <text class="fn-sub" x="450" y="74" text-anchor="middle">severity · context</text>
        </g>
        <g class="flow-node">
          <rect x="545" y="34" width="160" height="56" rx="7"></rect>
          <text x="625" y="58" text-anchor="middle">Dedupe</text>
          <text class="fn-sub" x="625" y="74" text-anchor="middle">already open?</text>
        </g>
        <g class="flow-node">
          <rect x="720" y="34" width="160" height="56" rx="7"></rect>
          <text x="800" y="58" text-anchor="middle">Open case</text>
          <text class="fn-sub" x="800" y="74" text-anchor="middle">assigned to owner</text>
        </g>

        <circle id="flow-token" cx="0" cy="62" r="5"></circle>
      </svg>

      <div class="flow-grid">
        <div>
          <p class="possess-label">what it cost</p>
          <div class="flow-clock" data-flow-clock>·</div>
          <p class="flow-cost" data-flow-cost></p>
          <ol class="flow-steps" data-flow-steps>
            <li><span class="t"></span><span class="n"></span></li>
            <li><span class="t"></span><span class="n"></span></li>
            <li><span class="t"></span><span class="n"></span></li>
            <li><span class="t"></span><span class="n"></span></li>
            <li><span class="t"></span><span class="n"></span></li>
          </ol>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="possess-label">the alert, as it travels</p>
          <pre class="code-out" data-flow-payload aria-live="polite"></pre>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="flow-compare" data-flow-compare hidden>
        <div class="flow-compare-grid">
          <div>
            <p class="possess-label">automated &middot; Torq</p>
            <pre class="code-out" data-cmp-auto></pre>
          </div>
          <div>
            <p class="possess-label">by hand &middot; an analyst</p>
            <pre class="code-out" data-cmp-manual></pre>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="flow-verdict">
          <span class="flow-vt" data-cmp-auto-t>·</span>
          <span class="flow-vs">vs</span>
          <span class="flow-vt slow" data-cmp-manual-t>·</span>
          <span class="flow-ratio" data-cmp-ratio></span>
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="sample-note">
        Illustrative timings and a redacted resource; the shape of the workflow
        is the real one. Both runs end with the same payload on purpose: the
        workflow does nothing an analyst could not do by hand. It does it in
        seconds, at 3 am, for every alert, without mistyping the project id. The
        owner lookup in step two only works because of the mapping in case study
        02.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Panel>

  <SectionHead label="Case study 05" title="Prevention over reaction" />
  <p>
    <strong>In one line:</strong> I recommended GCP Organization Policy
    constraints that block risky configurations at creation, moving recurring
    misconfigurations from fix-after-detection to never-created.
  </p>
  <p>
    CSPM tells you what is already misconfigured: useful, but always after the
    fact. Two levers decide what one bad config costs: whether it can be created
    at all, and whether its finding can reach an owner. Toggle them and deploy.
  </p>

  <Panel label="exposure window · detect vs prevent" status="illustrative">
    <div class="prevent-demo" data-clock>
      <div class="map-controls">
        <button class="cs-switch" type="button" data-policy aria-pressed="false">org policy: not enforced</button>
        <button class="cs-switch" type="button" data-cmdb aria-pressed="true">cmdb owner: mapped</button>
        <span class="sep"></span>
        <button class="cs-run" type="button" data-deploy>▶ deploy a public bucket</button>
      </div>

      <div class="clock-wrap">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 900 110" role="img" aria-label="Exposure window on a fixed three-week scale">
          <g class="exp-grid">
            <line x1="30" y1="76" x2="880" y2="76"></line>
            <line x1="30" y1="70" x2="30" y2="82"></line>
            <text x="30" y="18">0</text>
            <line x1="70" y1="70" x2="70" y2="82"></line>
            <text x="64" y="18">1d</text>
            <line x1="111" y1="70" x2="111" y2="82"></line>
            <text x="105" y="18">2d</text>
            <line x1="313" y1="70" x2="313" y2="82"></line>
            <text x="305" y="18">1w</text>
            <line x1="596" y1="70" x2="596" y2="82"></line>
            <text x="588" y="18">2w</text>
            <line x1="880" y1="70" x2="880" y2="82"></line>
            <text x="880" y="18" text-anchor="end">3w</text>
          </g>

          <rect class="exp-lane" x="30" y="54" width="850" height="16" rx="4"></rect>
          <rect id="exp-bar" x="30" y="54" width="0" height="16" rx="4"></rect>

          <g data-events></g>

          <g class="exp-denied">
            <text x="40" y="66">DENIED at creation: nothing exists to be exposed</text>
          </g>

          <g id="exp-head">
            <line x1="0" y1="46" x2="0" y2="78"></line>
            <rect class="exp-caret" x="1" y="56" width="5" height="12"></rect>
          </g>
        </svg>
      </div>

      <div class="exp-readout">
        <span class="exp-window" data-exp-window>·</span>
        <span class="exp-sub" data-exp-sub></span>
      </div>

      <p class="sample-note">
        Timings are illustrative; the mechanics are real. One fixed three-week
        scale for every scenario, so the bars compare honestly. The constraint
        is evaluated at creation, which is why the prevented case has no bar at
        all rather than a short one.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Panel>

  <SectionHead label="Also" title="GRC, audit and reporting" />
  <ul class="cs-facts">
    <li>
      <strong>Reporting:</strong> automated the weekly security metrics report
      with scripts and macros, from a multi-hour manual process to minutes.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Audit readiness:</strong> I facilitate the annual cloud control
      reviews; governance docs stay audit-ready through CI/CD-driven updates.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>First rotation (8 months):</strong> security risk remediation and
      issue tracking in ServiceNow GRC.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Internship (2023):</strong> browser extension governance with
      Active Directory; risk exceptions in ServiceNow and ZenGRC.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <script is:inline src="/js/field.js" defer></script>
  <script is:inline src="/js/soar-flow.js" defer></script>
  <script is:inline src="/js/exposure-clock.js" defer></script>
</ProjectLayout>
